      <div class="kpi-label">Top Emerging Topic MoM Growth<br><small>(E. coli research)</small></div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a5276 0%, #2980b9 100%);">
      <div class="kpi-value">3,924</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Publications in 2024 (80.3%)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1e8449 0%, #27ae60 100%);">
      <div class="kpi-value">737</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Publications in 2025 (15.1%)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #b7600a 0%, #e67e22 100%);">
      <div class="kpi-value">225</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Publications in 2026 (4.6%)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Publication Volume -->
<div class="section" id="volume">
  <h2>2. Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</h2>

  <p>The temporal distribution of publications reveals a pronounced skew toward 2024, which accounts for 80.3% of all records. This is a known artifact of PubMed's indexing pipeline: older publications accumulate more fully indexed entries over time, while recent publications (especially from late 2025 and early 2026) are still being processed. Accordingly, <strong>raw count comparisons across years should be interpreted as reflecting indexing lag rather than actual research output decline</strong>.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="chartMonthlyVolume"></canvas>
  </div>
  <p class="footnote">Note: 754 records with unknown publication months are excluded from monthly trends. The Jan-2024 spike (1,665 publications) likely reflects both a genuine seasonal pattern and backfill indexing.</p>

  <h3>Yearly Distribution</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share</th><th>Known-Month Records</th><th>Unknown-Month Records</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td>3,924</td><td>80.3%</td><td>3,349</td><td>575</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2025</td><td>737</td><td>15.1%</td><td>558</td><td>179</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2026</td><td>225</td><td>4.6%</td><td>225</td><td>0</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Quarterly Trends</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="chartQuarterly"></canvas>
  </div>
  <p>The quarterly view smooths monthly volatility and reveals that once the Q1 2024 indexing backlog is excluded, publication volumes remain relatively stable from Q2 2024 through Q4 2025, ranging from 119 to 545 records per quarter. The Q4 2025 uptick (169 records) and strong Q1 2026 showing (225 records) may signal either a genuine acceleration or continued indexing catch-up.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Methodological note:</strong> Any analysis of growth or decline over this window must account for the fact that 2024 data has had substantially more time to accumulate MeSH indexing, DOIs, and other metadata. The apparent decline from 2024 to 2026 is primarily an artifact, not a real-world contraction in research output.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Geographic Landscape -->
<div class="section" id="geographic">
  <h2>3. Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>

  <p>China and the USA dominate the dataset, together contributing 33.8% of all publications. The top 10 countries account for 53.6% of total output, while the remaining 46.4% is distributed across over 90 nations &mdash; indicating a long tail of smaller contributing countries.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="chartCountryBar"></canvas>
  </div>
  <p class="footnote">Country names were consolidated (e.g., "PR China" and "People's Republic of China" mapped to "China"; "USA" and "United States" mapped to "USA"). Hong Kong and Jiangxi Province records were attributed to China. 144 records had unknown country affiliations.</p>

  <h3>Top 15 Countries by Publication Count</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share</th><th>2024</th><th>2025</th><th>2026</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>China</td><td>1,122</td><td>23.0%</td><td>936</td><td>102</td><td>47</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>USA</td><td>539</td><td>11.0%</td><td>443</td><td>74</td><td>22</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>India</td><td>196</td><td>4.0%</td><td>129</td><td>59</td><td>8</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Japan</td><td>137</td><td>2.8%</td><td>113</td><td>18</td><td>6</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Iran</td><td>108</td><td>2.2%</td><td>78</td><td>27</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>South Korea</td><td>108</td><td>2.2%</td><td>90</td><td>12</td><td>6</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>Canada</td><td>107</td><td>2.2%</td><td>89</td><td>15</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>UK</td><td>100</td><td>2.0%</td><td>87</td><td>11</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>Germany</td><td>98</td><td>2.0%</td><td>82</td><td>12</td><td>4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>Italy</td><td>89</td><td>1.8%</td><td>81</td><td>8</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>11</td><td>Australia</td><td>65</td><td>1.3%</td><td>51</td><td>11</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12</td><td>Brazil</td><td>61</td><td>1.2%</td><td>49</td><td>9</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>13</td><td>Spain</td><td>60</td><td>1.2%</td><td>51</td><td>8</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>14</td><td>France</td><td>50</td><td>1.0%</td><td>40</td><td>8</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>15</td><td>Poland</td><td>38</td><td>0.8%</td><td>32</td><td>4</td><td>0</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Geographic Shifts Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="chartCountryYear"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key observation:</strong> The top 10 countries' share of total publications is gradually declining (54.3% in 2024, 52.9% in 2025, 48.4% in 2026), suggesting a modest diversification of the research base. India's share notably rises from 3.3% in 2024 to 8.0% in 2025 to 3.6% in 2026 (small-N volatility). However, 2026 figures are based on only 225 records and should be treated with caution.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Topic & MeSH Analysis -->
<div class="section" id="topics">
  <h2>4. Research Topic &amp; MeSH Keyword Analysis</h2>

  <p>MeSH major topics are available for 2,583 of 4,886 records (52.9%). Missing MeSH data is concentrated among newer publications that have not yet been fully indexed. Among indexed records, <strong>COVID-19 remains the single most prevalent major topic</strong>, though it shows clear deceleration over the observation window.</p>

