<!-- Key Takeaways -->
<div class="section" id="takeaways">
  <h2>7. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>

  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>1. Indexing lag distorts temporal interpretation &mdash; normalize accordingly.</strong><br>
    The 80%/15%/5% split across 2024/2025/2026 is primarily a PubMed indexing artifact, not a signal of declining research output. Policy analysts should use rates and normalized metrics rather than raw counts when comparing years. The apparent steep drop after Q1 2024 is largely backfill-related.
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>2. China's research volume is dominant, but its OA rate lags behind peers.</strong><br>
    China contributes 23.0% of all publications &mdash; more than double the USA's 11.0% &mdash; yet its open-access rate (54.5%) is the lowest among top-15 producers. This gap is a significant policy lever. Funders and institutions pushing for open science should prioritize engagement with Chinese research institutions and publishers to close this gap.
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>3. COVID-19 research is declining rapidly; emerging topics are diversifying.</strong><br>
    COVID-19 remains the #1 major topic overall, but with a -16.9% average monthly decline rate, it is clearly on the wane. Fast-rising topics include <em>E. coli</em> research (+44.4% MoM), long noncoding RNA (+33.3%), and non-small-cell lung carcinoma (+16.7%). Funding strategists should monitor these areas for sustained momentum.
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>4. Open access is trending upward, but progress is uneven.</strong><br>
    The overall OA rate of 65.1% represents substantial adoption, with 2025 reaching 74.1%. However, this progress is driven by fully OA mega-journals (Scientific Reports, Heliyon, Cureus) rather than systemic change across all journals. Traditional subscription-based journals still account for a large share of closed-access publications.
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>5. The geographic research base is slowly diversifying.</strong><br>
    The top 10 countries' share has declined from 54.3% (2024) to 48.4% (2026), suggesting that research output is gradually spreading to a broader set of nations. India's rising share (3.3% to 8.0% in 2025) is particularly notable and may warrant increased funding attention.
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>6. Collaboration intensity varies significantly by country and research type.</strong><br>
    Systematic reviews, RCTs, and preprints show median author counts of 8, indicating high-collaboration research designs. Italy leads in team size (median 8), while India and Iran operate with smaller teams (median 5). Funders seeking to promote multi-institutional research should consider these baselines when designing collaboration incentives.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Methodology -->
<div class="section" id="methodology">
  <h2>8. Methodology &amp; Data Notes</h2>

  <h3>Data Source</h3>
  <p>The dataset comprises 4,886 biomedical research abstracts sourced from NCBI PubMed, published between January 2024 and March 2026. Data was obtained from Kaggle (kanchana1990/biomedical-research-abstracts-20242026) and underwent prior QA/QC including deduplication by PMID and title, garbage text removal, abstract length filtering (50&ndash;1,000 words), and date-range scope filtering.</p>

  <h3>Data Cleaning Applied</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Country consolidation:</strong> Variants such as "PR China", "People's Republic of China", "P R China", and "Jiangxi Province" were mapped to "China"; "United States" and "United States of America" mapped to "USA"; "Republic of Korea" mapped to "South Korea"; "Hong Kong" mapped to "China".</li>
    <li><strong>Unknown months:</strong> 754 records with pub_month = "Unknown" (pub_month_num = 0) were excluded from monthly and quarterly trend analyses but included in yearly aggregates.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing MeSH data:</strong> 2,303 records (47.1%) lack keywords and major_topic values, consistent with indexing lag for newer publications. Topic analyses are restricted to the 2,583 records with available major_topic data.</li>
    <li><strong>Research type parsing:</strong> The research_type field contains semicolon-separated values. Individual types were extracted for frequency analysis; the first non-funding-related type was used as the "primary type" for per-record classification.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The dataset represents a subset of PubMed and may not be fully representative of all biomedical research output globally.</li>
    <li>Temporal comparisons are confounded by PubMed's indexing lag, which disproportionately affects 2025 and 2026 records.</li>
    <li>Country attribution is based on the first author's affiliation only, which may undercount multi-national collaborations.</li>
    <li>Emerging topic growth rates are computed from small monthly counts (single-digit publications per topic per month) and are therefore subject to high volatility. They should be interpreted as directional signals rather than precise estimates.</li>
    <li>The 2026 data covers only January&ndash;March, making year-level comparisons with 2024 (full 12 months) misleading without normalization.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

